cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it using the metadata in this file."
title: "Revisiting exact-penalty linear programming: a projection-path walker for the regime where Newton slows down"
type: software
version: 0.1.0-draft
authors:
  - family-names: Kline
    given-names: Jeffery
repository-code: "https://github.com/jeff-kline/exact-penalty-lp-walker"
url: "https://github.com/jeff-kline/exact-penalty-lp-walker"
license: GPL-3.0-only
abstract: >-
  A linear program can be solved exactly by minimizing a quadratic penalty at a
  finite penalty parameter. A Newton method built on that fact performs well
  on very tall systems and slows sharply when constraints outnumber variables
  by less than about 2 or 3, a band that holds every Netlib model once
  converted to the inequality form used here. This repository contains a
  complementary method, the
  t-walker, which follows the exact dual projection path from face to face
  rather than re-solving the penalty problem at a sequence of penalty values,
  together with the C++ implementation, benchmark harness, canonical Netlib
  fixtures, and frozen measurement records needed to check the reported
  timings and accuracy under a single original-data certificate.
keywords:
  - linear programming
  - quadratic penalty
  - parametric quadratic programming
  - active-set method
  - Newton method
  - projection path
